--- import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro"; import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro"; import "../../styles/standards-poster.css"; import "../../styles/extended.css"; import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj13.json"; --- <StandardPageLayout code={metadata.code} standardType={metadata.framework} title={metadata.title} description={metadata.description} issued={metadata.issued} latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment} status={metadata.status} summary={metadata.summary} bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice} > <Fragment slot="toc"> <PageToc items={[{"id":"s1","label":"Conceptual Framework Linkage"},{"id":"s2","label":"Scope — the Historical Dual Classification Model"},{"id":"s3","label":"Finance Leases — Two Sub-Categories"},{"id":"s4","label":"Operating Leases — Off-Balance-Sheet Treatment"},{"id":"s5","label":"THE REFORM — ASBJ Statement No. 34's Single On-Balance-Sheet Model"},{"id":"s6","label":"Worked Example — the Old Finance Lease Treatment"},{"id":"s7","label":"Worked Example — the Old Operating Lease Treatment"},{"id":"s8","label":"Current Treatment — ASBJ Statement No. 34"},{"id":"s9","label":"Disclosure Requirements (Current, Post-Transition)"},{"id":"s10","label":"Critical Judgments"},{"id":"s11","label":"Relationship to IAS/IFRS — Then and Now"},{"id":"s12","label":"Statement Mapping"},{"id":"s13","label":"Related Standards"},{"id":"s14","label":"Glossary"}]} /> </Fragment> <div class="wrap"> <div class="callout warn">SUPERSEDED STATUS: This Statement's dual finance/operating lessee classification model has been SUPERSEDED by ASBJ Statement No. 34, Leases (issued 2024), which introduces a single, largely on-balance-sheet lessee model aligned in spirit with IFRS 16. This poster explains the historical model this Statement established and the direction of the reform toward No. 34; consult ASBJ's current materials for the precise effective date and transition provisions.</div> <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A dual-classification model, now moving toward a single on-balance-sheet approach</div><div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 13 HISTORICALLY established Japan's lessee accounting model — DISTINGUISHING "FINANCE LEASES" (recognised ON the Balance Sheet, reflecting the economics of essentially FINANCING an asset purchase) from "OPERATING LEASES" (kept OFF the Balance Sheet, with rental expense recognised on a STRAIGHT-LINE basis) — broadly SIMILAR in STRUCTURE to the OLD international standard IAS 17. This Statement has since been SUPERSEDED by <strong>ASBJ Statement No. 34, Leases</strong>, which moves TOWARD a single, largely ON-BALANCE-SHEET lessee model conceptually ALIGNED with IFRS 16.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — the Historical Dual Classification Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">All companies entering lease arrangements</div><div class="principle">This Statement HISTORICALLY applied to ALL companies entering LEASE arrangements as either lessee or lessor, requiring CLASSIFICATION of each lease as either a FINANCE lease or an OPERATING lease based on the SUBSTANCE of the arrangement.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Finance Leases — Two Sub-Categories</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Ownership-transfer vs non-ownership-transfer finance leases</div><div class="th cols-1-2"><span>Sub-category</span><span>Depreciation basis</span></div> <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Ownership-transfer finance lease</span><span>Depreciated over the LEASED ASSET's OWN estimated useful life — treated much like an OUTRIGHT purchase</span></div> <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Non-ownership-transfer finance lease</span><span>Depreciated over the LEASE TERM itself, reflecting that ownership will NOT ultimately transfer to the lessee</span> <div class="note">This SUB-CLASSIFICATION within finance leases was a DISTINCTIVE, genuinely J-GAAP-SPECIFIC nuance not present in the equivalent old IAS 17 finance lease category, which used a SINGLE depreciation approach regardless of an ownership-transfer distinction.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Operating Leases — Off-Balance-Sheet Treatment</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Straight-line rental expense, no asset or liability recognised</div><div class="text-supporting">Leases NOT meeting the finance lease criteria were classified as OPERATING leases — with NO asset or liability recognised on the lessee's Balance Sheet, and rental payments recognised as an EXPENSE on a STRAIGHT-LINE basis over the lease term — the OLD "off-balance-sheet" treatment now ELIMINATED (for the vast majority of leases) under the successor standard, ASBJ Statement No. 34.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>THE REFORM — ASBJ Statement No. 34's Single On-Balance-Sheet Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Bringing substantially all leases onto the lessee's Balance Sheet</div><div class="text-supporting">ASBJ Statement No. 34, issued in 2024, ELIMINATES the OLD finance/operating DUAL classification for LESSEES — requiring the recognition of a "RIGHT-OF-USE ASSET" and a corresponding LEASE LIABILITY for SUBSTANTIALLY ALL leases, broadly MIRRORING IFRS 16's headline reform internationally, though with SIMPLIFICATIONS appropriate to J-GAAP's own practice. This represents a GENUINELY MAJOR shift in Japanese lease accounting, eliminating the LONG-STANDING off-balance-sheet operating lease treatment this Statement (No. 13) had PREVIOUSLY provided.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — the Old Finance Lease Treatment</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">On-balance-sheet recognition under the historical model</div> <div class="worked"><div class="wk-title">Example: A non-ownership-transfer finance lease of manufacturing equipment</div><div class="wk-body">Under the OLD regime, a company enters a 5-year lease of manufacturing equipment classified as a NON-OWNERSHIP-TRANSFER finance lease, with a present value of lease payments of ¥50,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company recognised a ¥50,000,000 LEASE ASSET and a corresponding ¥50,000,000 LEASE LIABILITY on the Balance Sheet, DEPRECIATING the asset over the 5-YEAR LEASE TERM (since ownership does not transfer) — broadly SIMILAR in effect to how the SAME lease would be treated under TODAY's ASBJ Statement No. 34.</div></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — the Old Operating Lease Treatment</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Off-balance-sheet treatment, now eliminated</div> <div class="worked"><div class="wk-title">Example: A short office space rental, under the old regime</div><div class="wk-body">Under the OLD regime, a company leases office space under an arrangement classified as an OPERATING lease, with annual rent of ¥12,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">UNDER THE OLD STANDARD, NO asset or liability appeared on the Balance Sheet — the company simply recognised ¥12,000,000 of RENTAL EXPENSE each year, straight-line. UNDER TODAY's ASBJ Statement No. 34, the SAME lease would generally now require recognition of a right-of-use ASSET and a LEASE LIABILITY, REPRESENTING a MATERIAL change in Balance Sheet presentation for this type of arrangement.</div></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Current Treatment — ASBJ Statement No. 34</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Today's authoritative leases framework</div><div class="text-supporting">Companies TODAY apply <strong>ASBJ Statement No. 34, Leases</strong>, recognising right-of-use assets and lease liabilities for SUBSTANTIALLY ALL leases (subject to limited exceptions, e.g. short-term or low-value leases, broadly analogous to IFRS 16's own exceptions) — REPRESENTING the CURRENT, comprehensive successor to this Statement's historical dual-classification model.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Current, Post-Transition)</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What applies today instead</div><div class="check-list"><span>Right-of-use assets and lease liabilities recognised under ASBJ Statement No. 34</span><span>Maturity analysis of lease liabilities and information about short-term/low-value lease exemptions elected</span></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most (historically and today)</div> <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">HISTORICAL JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Classifying a lease as finance or operating, and whether ownership transfer was substantively expected</span></div></div> <div><div class="elabel">CURRENT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Determining the lease term, discount rate, and whether short-term/low-value exemptions apply under ASBJ Statement No. 34</span></div></div></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS — Then and Now</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison across the reform</div><div class="th cols-12-12-12"><span>Feature</span><span>Old J-GAAP (this Statement) / New (No. 34)</span><span>IFRS</span></div> <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Historical model</span><span>Dual finance/operating lessee classification</span><span>OLD IAS 17 — same dual classification structure</span></div> <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Current model</span><span>ASBJ Statement No. 34 — single on-balance-sheet model</span><span>IFRS 16 — single on-balance-sheet model, converged in overall approach</span> <div class="note">Japan's lease accounting reform TRACKS the SAME broad direction IFRS itself took (IAS 17 → IFRS 16), though on its OWN separate timeline — for an EXTENDED period, J-GAAP's lease model REMAINED at the "old IAS 17" stage even AFTER IFRS moved on, mirroring a pattern seen elsewhere in this poster series (e.g. UK GAAP's FRS 102 leases).</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where lease accounting now lands</div><div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div> <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Lease recognition</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Governed entirely by the current ASBJ Statement No. 34</span></div> </div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div><div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div> <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 34</span><span>Successor, comprehensive standard — the current authoritative J-GAAP leases framework</span></div> </div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cn span2"> <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Ownership-transfer finance lease (legacy term)</div><div class="glossary-def">A finance lease where ownership of the leased asset is expected to transfer to the lessee, depreciated over the asset's own useful life under the old regime.</div></div> <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Right-of-use asset</div><div class="glossary-def">The asset recognised by a lessee under ASBJ Statement No. 34, representing its right to use a leased asset over the lease term.</div></div> </div> </div><div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><span class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</span></div></div> </div> </StandardPageLayout> 